<section class="section">
  <h2>What it does</h2>
  <p class="section-intro">
    A chat app and tool-calling loop for a model you already have: one you run
    yourself with Ollama or LM Studio, or one behind an API such as OpenAI or
    OpenRouter. It connects to MCP servers for tools, resources and prompts, and
    keeps its settings, servers, sessions and every message in Postgres. One
    front end ships to the browser, Android, and a Windows or Linux desktop app.
  </p>

  <ul class="card-grid">
    <li>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>Any OpenAI-compatible model</h3>
        <p>
          Point it at a base URL, pick a model from the list the server returns,
          and optionally give a small fast model the side jobs: session titles,
          context compaction, tool preselection and follow-up suggestions.
        </p>
      </article>
    </li>
    <li>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>MCP tools, loaded on demand</h3>
        <p>
          Stdio and HTTP MCP servers plug in from the settings screen, and by
          default the model gets a name-only catalogue and loads the tool
          definitions it needs instead of being sent every schema on every
          request.
        </p>
      </article>
    </li>
    <li>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>What every turn cost</h3>
        <p>
          Each reply reports its output tokens, tokens per second, time to first
          token and total time, beside a context meter and a breakdown of what
          filled the window.
        </p>
      </article>
    </li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="section" id="start">
  <h2>Get started</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <pre class="code"><code>git clone https://github.com/cubicecho/min-agent
cd min-agent
docker compose up -d</code></pre>
    <p>
      That builds the image and starts it with its Postgres database. Open
      <code>http://localhost:8787</code>, go to Settings → Agent, enter the base
      URL of an OpenAI-compatible server, refresh the model list, pick a model
      and save.
    </p>
    <p>
      Nothing in min-agent is authenticated, so publish the port only on a
      network you trust.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p class="readme-link">
    Full documentation lives in the project README:
    <a href="{{ site.repo }}#readme" rel="noopener">{{ site.repo }}</a>
  </p>
</section>
